<P>
<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
Over the years I have paid my dues when it comes to computer baseball games.  It all started back in the early 1980s with the Atari 2600’s baseball cartridge.  After that I recall a Commodore 64 baseball game that had pretty good graphics (for the time) but was slow to play.  Finally, along came the Sega Genesis and Tony LaRussa baseball.  I found the game good, but it left something wanting.  Can Triple Play baseball erase all of these bad memories?  You’ll have to read on to find out.
<P>
When we received our copy of Triple Play, I was eager to get it installed and to start playing.  Alas, I was first forced to attend a meeting of the WEWP staff…if you’ve ever attended a meeting when you’d rather be somewhere else, then you can sympathize with me.  This meeting was different.  After we had finished our technical discussions (fifteen minutes) we broke out the Triple Play Play Station version and proceeded to challenge each other in the Big League Challenge (home run derby). This only made my anticipation of the PC version of the game that much greater.  After playing a few games of this I made my excuse, grabbed my copy of Triple Play and hurried home.
<p>
Installation of the game went smoothly and after restarting my computer I found myself staring at the Main Menu.  Since I had just come off playing the Play Station version of Big League Challenge I thought it would be fun to play the PC version and compare them.  I soon found myself playing the part of slugging Sammy Sosa and the computer swung the bat for Troy Glaus.  It was no contest, Sosa won hands down.  I guess before I go any further, I should explain the basics behind the game for everyone.
<p>
Triple Play baseball allows the player to choose from a number of different game modes.  You can choose to play a single game, a season, the Big League Challenge, or draft a new league.  Of these options, the Big League Challenge is the one that is not self-explanatory.  In the BLC you slug it out with other MLB stars to determine who is the king of pop.  This can be done in a tournament format- which pits 12 (official) or 16 players against each other in head to head rounds, One-on-one format- which takes two batters and the winner is the one who hits the most dingers, finally you have the Extreme format (which I found to be the most fun).
<p>
In the Extreme format the players selects two players he/she would like to battle each other.  Once the hitters have been selected the player selects the field (there are four) this contest will take place in.  This is what makes this format so fun.  Each of these fields has a variety of “Targets” that the batter can hit for extra points.  The default field is Cashman Field in Las Vegas where a similar event occurred in February 2001.  However, of the four fields this one was the most basic.   I am partial to the “Living Room” stadium where your players are miniaturized and their home runs can hit the grandfather clock, the fireplace, framed pictures, the dog, and a big ugly bird.  I had a player get over 12000 points in the event with only four home runs (he hit the bird twice).
<p>
The Season play feature allows the player to create a customized season to play.  The different options that are customizable by the player include:
<p>
Season length (15, 30, 60, or 162 games)
Playoff length- the player selects the number of games for the three playoff rounds (1-1-1,1-1-3, 3-3-5, or 5-7-7).
Difficulty (Rookie, Pro, or All-Star)
The number of innings (1,3,5,7,or 9) per game.
Points cap play- when ON each team is allocated 1875 points (which might already be used up by the current value of players on the roster) with which they can purchase draft picks, trades, or selections from the season store (you can buy things like improved running, a new stadium, etc.).
Smart trades- when ON the CPU offers trades during the season and makes trades with other CPU controlled teams during the season.
Draft- when on the player participates in a draft (the computer can conduct the draft for you).
<p>

On to game play.  If you are playing a single game or attempting to take your favorite team (or in my case my favorite player’s team) to the World Series you will not be disappointed with the game.  The controls, once you learn the different button combinations, are easy to remember and even easier to use.  Although, I wouldn’t want to play the game without some sort of game pad- the keyboard commands would make this game a little harder to master.  If I could improve anything it would be the manner in which substitutes (pinch hitters or relievers) are brought into the game.  There are times when this seems confusing and a little too difficult to do.
<p>
The graphics are great.  You can actually recognize players (although some of them really only because of their batting stance).  You can read the names of the back of uniforms and each stadium is painstakingly recreated.  When you hit a home run there are multiple camera angles that will show you just how much you managed to mangle the ball.  The pre and post game graphics are great.  Before the game you can watch the pitcher warming up and see players running around the field.  After the game you see the winning team congratulating each other.  
<p>
The sound is incredible.  First of all you have Jim Hughson doing the play-by-play along with Buck Martinez providing color commentary which is great.  A couple of the quotes from this duo will make you laugh during Extreme play.  Buck and Jim even fill in some of the dead air with mid-game ads from sponsors.  The in game sounds are fantastic.  You can hear individual fans cheering (or booing) players on.  You can hear the vendors hawking their wares and you can hear the PA announcer warning people about the “dangers” of attending an MLB game or paging a particular patron.  Along with the “normal” baseball sounds there is a soundtrack that plays during the game.  At first I was ambivalent to this music.  However, the more I played the game the more I came to understand that this soundtrack adds to the overall experience of playing the game.
<p>
Overall, I found this game to be of exceptional caliber.  The graphics and sound alone make it worth taking a look at.  If you are at all interested in what could have been the “Draft” option can make playing a season that much more interesting by creating your own teams.
